<div class="top">
  <div><h1>SignalRx: finding what pharma filings don't say on their own</h1>
    <div class="small">Autonomous financial research agent · <b>Track D: Pharma &amp; Healthcare</b> · Finding the Next Signal, ATMOS 2026</div></div>
  <div class="meta"><b>{{ team }}</b><br>{{ members }}<br>MVP: {{ repo }}</div>
</div>

<p class="lede"><b>Idea.</b> In pharma and healthcare the important story usually sits <i>between</i> documents, not inside one:
an FDA inspection at a contract plant the company doesn't own, a government payor's billing order, a loss-of-exclusivity date,
a partner's filing, a peer's results for the same quarter. SignalRx builds a dated <b>evidence ledger</b> across these
independent source families, lets the numbers say <i>where</i> something moved, sends an LLM investigator to find <i>why</i>, and
then tries to <b>kill its own hypothesis</b> before reporting anything. A summariser reads one source; SignalRx reports only
findings that need at least two independent families and calculations no single source states.</p>

<h2>MVP pilot results (working code, replayable; all pass the output verifier)</h2>
<table>
<tr><th style="width:12%">Company</th><th>Discovery the agent produced (abridged)</th><th style="width:7%">Brief</th><th style="width:8%">Strength</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Dr. Reddy's</b></td><td>North America is down to <b>27.4%</b> of revenue (Q1FY27), <b>below even FY21's 37.8%</b>, while ex-NA revenue grew <b>+23.5%</b> in FY26. The lenalidomide cliff is already in the numbers, so <b>semaglutide is now the swing factor</b>, and it has slipped twice in 8 months: a Health Canada notice (Oct-25), then an out-of-spec API (Jul-26), leading to a ₹240 cr write-down (<b>54% of Q1 profit</b>) and a pen plan cut from 12M to 6–7M.</td><td>D1/D3</td><td><span class="pill">Strong</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Cipla</b></td><td>The sharpest US decline came from <b>a plant Cipla doesn't own</b>: the exclusive lanreotide maker (Pharmathen, Greece) closed an FDA inspection with 9 observations on 21-Nov-25. NA then fell <b>28% QoQ</b> ($233M→$167M) in a quarter that <b>closed 14 days before Cipla disclosed it</b>. The skeptic shows lanreotide explains only <b>~51%</b> of the step, and the $1bn exit-rate target needs <b>+54%</b> with zero lanreotide.</td><td>D2</td><td><span class="pill">Strong</span></td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Max Healthcare</b></td><td>The H2-FY26 <b>ARPOB stall was a payor-policy shock, not demand</b>. After CGHS drug-billing rules, Max stopped high-value chemo for institutional patients, and oncology fell from <b>26% to 22%</b> of inpatient revenue. Reported growth (16%) understates the core (20%), ~1.2pp of the Q1 ARPOB rebound is the CGHS tariff revision, and the "normalisation" date is just the base effect lapping. <i>Secondary: the bed target needs 3.3–3.7× the current build pace.</i></td><td>D2</td><td><span class="pill m">Moderate</span></td></tr>
</table>

<div class="cols">
<h2>Workflow: company → plan → evidence → discovery</h2>
<div class="flow"><span>resolve</span><i>→</i><span>harvest</span><i>→</i><span>verify inputs</span><i>→</i><span>profile</span><i>→</i><span>scan</span><i>→</i><span>plan</span><i>→</i><span class="l">investigate</span><i>→</i><span class="l">expand 3rd parties</span><i>→</i><span>hypothesise</span><i>→</i><span>skeptic</span><i>→</i><span class="l">adversarial search</span><i>→</i><span>score</span><i>→</i><span class="l">write</span><i>→</i><span>verify output</span><i>→</i><span>render</span></div>
<p class="small">Blue = deterministic code; orange = LLM. The LLM <b>proposes</b>; code <b>disposes</b> (it computes, checks and scores).</p>
<ol>
<li><b>Harvest</b>: 5–10 years of statements and quarterly results, Reg-30 filings, FDA recalls/approvals, dated headlines.</li>
<li><b>Scan</b>: where the numbers moved: mix shifts, segment step-downs, reported-vs-adjusted growth gaps, ARPOB stalls, announced-vs-commissioned capacity.</li>
<li><b>Plan</b>: ranks the Track D angle library (D1 model drift, D2 regulatory-before-coverage, D3 geography, plus third-party plants, payor policy, capacity, narrative gap) by archetype fit × anomalies, with one open LLM-proposed slot.</li>
<li><b>Investigate</b>: a Claude tool loop per angle (search, fetch, PDF search, FDA/NSE/news). Each record needs a verbatim quote from a fetched document <b>with exactly matching numbers</b>, or it is rejected.</li>
<li><b>Expand</b>: each third party found (contract maker, supplier, payor) gets its own regulator probe. This is how the Cipla finding is reached.</li>
<li><b>Hypothesise → challenge → score → write</b>, then the verifier re-traces every number in the prose.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Generalises across the track, with no company tuning</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Universe from NSE index files</b> at run time (today Sun, Cipla, Dr. Reddy's, Apollo, Max): reshuffles need no code.</li>
<li><b>Archetype from the company's own text and segments</b> (formulations / API-CDMO / hospital / diagnostics) routes to the right KPIs. The pilots cover two archetypes on one code path, which the tie-break pool (Divi's, Lupin, Fortis, Syngene…) shares.</li>
<li><b>Sector knowledge pack, not company knowledge</b>: FDA/Health Canada/EU escalation ladders, a CGHS/NPPA/Sec-232/BIOSECURE watchlist, an LOE calendar and a controlled metric vocabulary. A test fails the build if any company name appears in code.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Triangulation and contradiction detection</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Source families</b> (company · each regulator · each counterparty · each publisher): a discovery needs ≥2, and the strength grade counts them.</li>
<li><b>Recompute, don't trust</b>: reported growth is recomputed from filed levels. In the pilot, one outlet printed Dr. Reddy's Q1 at <b>+6%</b> and NA at <b>+35%</b>; the filings give <b>−5.6%</b> and <b>−35.4%</b>, so both were dropped and logged.</li>
<li><b>Hierarchy</b>: regulator = filing &gt; transcript/counterparty &gt; broker/press &gt; aggregator &gt; blog. Overruled figures go to an audit log; derived values carry tolerance bands. Peers are compared for the same quarter.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Self-falsification and temporal reasoning</h2>
<p>The skeptic is code that <b>re-runs the numbers</b> for each alternative: sector-wide · acquired · one-off (adds it back) · temporary (dated fix becomes a falsifier) · second driver <i>with timing</i> · breadth · low-margin · already-in-headlines · data conflicts. Live runs also send an <b>adversarial investigator</b> after the leading hypothesis. Each event carries a date and a precision; the agent measures regulator → disclosure → headline → results lags. From the pilots:</p>
<ul>
<li>Cipla: the lenalidomide LOE (31-Jan-26) lands <i>after</i> the step quarter, so the finding was resized to "~51% explained".</li>
<li>Dr. Reddy's: broad ex-NA growth reframed "concentrated replacement" into "semaglutide is the swing factor".</li>
<li>Max: "normalisation by Q3–Q4 FY27" falls 13 months after the change, which the agent flags as base-effect lapping, not recovery.</li>
</ul>
</div>
<div class="grid2">
<div>
<h2>Output: interactive dashboard plus PDF research note (same content)</h2>
<img class="thumb" src="data:image/png;base64,{{ thumb }}" alt="SignalRx dashboard, Cipla run">
<p class="small" style="margin-top:3px">Top to bottom: the discovery (one sentence, citation chips), key-number tiles, a chart with dated event markers, why it matters, the strongest counterargument, falsification conditions, a timeline, evidence strength with reasons, the skeptic table, the full evidence trail (stance, family, passage, link; filterable), every calculation with formula and inputs, the hypothesis board including rejected ideas, the audit log and the agent trace. <b>Every claim is one click from its passage and URL.</b> The PDF is the same page printed to A4.</p>

<h2>Evidence strength rubric (shown with its reasons)</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Strong</b>: ≥4–6 independent families incl. a primary filing, most skeptic tests survived, no refutation, all conflicts resolved.</li>
<li><b>Moderate</b>: ≥3 families incl. a primary filing, but key attribution rests on management commentary or the press.</li>
<li><b>Weak</b>: thin or single-family support, or a refuting test. A weak finding is still shown, with that label.</li>
</ul>
<p>The ranking score is 0.35 novelty + 0.30 evidence + 0.20 materiality + 0.15 robustness. Novelty rewards non-company families, a third-party record that <i>leads</i> the company's disclosure, and derived calculations; it is penalised when headlines already carry the core terms.</p>

